  "reasoning": "1. Frequent yeast infections are a classic clinical indicator of elevated blood glucose levels. Excess sugar in bodily tissues and fluids creates an ideal environment for fungal overgrowth, making recurrent candidiasis a primary red flag for diabetes.\n2. Excessive sweating and getting hot easily can result from diabetic autonomic neuropathy, which damages the nerves that regulate thermoregulation and sweat gland activity. Additionally, fluctuations in blood sugar levels (both highs and lows) commonly trigger sudden sweating and heat intolerance.\n3. Trouble breathing during exercise may stem from cardiovascular complications frequently associated with diabetes, such as impaired microcirculation, early-onset heart strain, or general metabolic inefficiency that limits oxygen utilization during physical exertion.\n4. When synthesizing these three key symptoms—recurrent fungal infections, abnormal sweating/thermoregulation, and exertional dyspnea—diabetes emerges as the most comprehensive and medically consistent diagnosis among the provided options.",
